# ktm (Ace 6) recovery-side touch enablement.
#
# 1) ODM hbp5 HAL daemon: the ODM-partition rc starts it, but under the
#    recovery policy the binary has no executable-domain transition, so
#    init's seclabel audit refuses it while the system is enforcing.
#    Enable the generic permissive switch (bootable/recovery's
#    init.recovery.aera-selinux.rc) so the audit becomes non-fatal and
#    the daemon can run; the daemon then completes its driver handshake.
#    (TWRP-style custom recoveries running permissive is the community
#    norm; for a confined-domain alternative see the git history:
#    a device sepolicy extension with a permissive HAL domain.)
# 2) The ODM rc creates /data/vendor/touchconfig and its XML configs with
#    mode 0777. Once the daemon is up and has loaded its configs, tighten
#    ownership/permissions so unrelated local processes cannot modify the
#    touch configuration.
# 3) HBP IRQ gate: the HBP framework (oplus_hbp_core) registers the
#    touch-00 IRQ at probe time and then disables it; the panel-resume /
#    daemon handshake that normally re-enables it never runs in recovery.
#    Open the device's kernel IRQ gate once AERA module loading is
#    complete so the kernel direct-report path works regardless.

on early-init
    setprop sys.aera.selinux.permissive true

on property:post.decrypt.modules=true
    write /sys/devices/platform/soc/soc:hbp@0/irq_enable 0,1

# Tighten the ODM rc's 0777 touch configuration after the daemon is up.
service aera-touchcfg-lock /system/bin/sh -c "chmod 0770 /data/vendor/touchconfig; chown system:system /data/vendor/touchconfig; find /data/vendor/touchconfig -maxdepth 1 -name '*.xml' -exec chmod 0660 {} + 2>/dev/null; find /data/vendor/touchconfig -maxdepth 1 -name '*.xml' -exec chown system:system {} + 2>/dev/null"
    class main
    oneshot
    user root
    group root system
    seclabel u:r:recovery:s0

on property:init.svc.vendor.touch-aidl-2=running
    start aera-touchcfg-lock
